#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111735} :activity/id #uuid "ec05fcce-5a67-533c-840a-322806556dbc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050714 :topic/id #uuid "60b97f00-f86a-5a4f-834e-1a83f8ee3cc2" :topic/math-academy-id 1453} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111872 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $P$ is a point in the first quadrant that lies on the unit circle. We can then draw a right triangle, where the hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point. \n\n![](images/4b/4bae3a49b5557df9e5b50280d744aa72048b2d224df042e103a6b5d156467de7.png)\n\nSince the hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle, its length must be equal to $1.$ And, since the triangle is a right triangle, the $(x,y)$ coordinates of any point on the unit circle in the first quadrant must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this to find the $y$-coordinate of a point given the $x$-coordinate, and vice-versa.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of a point $P$ in the first quadrant on the unit circle is $\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{3}{7}$ into the above and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t          \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty&= \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}}\\[5pt]\n\t        y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n\n}\n$$\n\nSince $P$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive. So we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $y=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4029eb2-0222-5d8b-a285-7f0089655736" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2718 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "91d91920-7585-54d5-a3bb-aa4f02c97c17" :step/math-academy-id 87629 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111736}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110456 :question/id #uuid "42313322-7535-5969-ad84-958449e48c1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3480"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049224}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510724} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335778}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "51014196-5277-5ae7-8d38-8cacaa66b4eb" :step/math-academy-id 87630 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111867 :tutorial/content "Recall that the coordinates of a point on the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ have the property\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf this point is in the first quadrant, we can substitute these values into the Pythagorean Identity from before to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last expression is known as the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity,** and is arguably the most important equation in trigonometry.\n\nThe Pythagorean trigonometric identity holds for *every* angle $\\theta,$ not just if $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant. In this lesson, we're focusing on the first quadrant only. We'll learn how to deal with angles in other quadrants in a future lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "f0d65e8f-ee17-505a-8a0c-a8b8c87f02e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2550 :tutorial/title "The Pythagorean Identity"} :step/id #uuid "13bf9525-3d5e-5ef0-b342-b104b3a91ae7" :step/math-academy-id 87633 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111738}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110457 :question/id #uuid "02c747c2-0eda-5c53-8e43-829f878dd278" :question/math-academy-id "e-3482"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050291}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511715} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562479}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"} :step/id #uuid "e5d4c5eb-0cce-5c47-afdd-6deeebc20fba" :step/math-academy-id 87634 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110458 :question/id #uuid "bbf71023-510c-54af-9d0c-4df6744e3007" :question/math-academy-id "e-3484"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511716} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541154}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"} :step/id #uuid "a7c20379-d544-52e6-9e90-0d619215daf5" :step/math-academy-id 87637 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111741}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110459 :question/id #uuid "ba6cc6a0-dbb8-58d2-9d02-c76310028d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3485"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510753} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541137}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"} :step/id #uuid "4b539236-de75-501f-ac26-46341ff46dac" :step/math-academy-id 87640}] :activity/title "Using the Pythagorean Identity in the First Quadrant" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110825}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562519} :activity/id #uuid "c59feace-40c5-5a98-a7eb-6db0b5b34eb0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050714 :topic/id #uuid "60b97f00-f86a-5a4f-834e-1a83f8ee3cc2" :topic/math-academy-id 1453} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b2b084c-2030-569c-b3db-c1f0bb240497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108674} {:answer/id #uuid "a1178c63-4702-5bdc-9e41-c3046acb6f63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108675} {:answer/id #uuid "d3dca092-57eb-5e07-8d0d-0d53376fcddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108676} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1a8afd-47ad-5ee5-aef2-253b8da8405f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108677} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b6b5a3-b768-5bb1-8d7e-9d7a7b3b6af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186108674} :answer-field/id #uuid "af420e31-aeb4-5e79-ae37-c4b53833e453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d39d8f8-c6eb-55ec-8766-ad9675623f6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37821" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d51a8e78f8b8c1f05e502bd98aed6a253799336765f1331d23e63ec637bcb448.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/43/438ce908c902696b480bf9791cae089be40f9e7b2552208e13e08405496b696c.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "0090e87d-d8ba-537b-bdb4-37a028de7d1c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dad645d-0415-5658-be31-35d722e7881e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561854} {:answer/id #uuid "b7802217-fcc7-5486-bbd8-0b67e0f11610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562042} {:answer/id #uuid "c86f1ffc-4ac3-5b6a-963c-de4bf912162e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562081} {:answer/id #uuid "e20fd379-fb1a-5dbe-a692-d26f272c9382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562139} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a827f2-e12d-53fa-8215-157177df99c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562181}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562081} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd1bfcef-f919-5cd5-b2a5-2c3687f0610d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b33de5dd-a44b-50a7-ac6a-5ef9965fa83e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65061" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4fdf6f8d819a4ee043b1cc3b86d795e3166ebfe181e0958246dc084c2f9c6fc.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e2/e2c83b965f7108bff3165531446cce43006a41b9742914e8fc40c50741230427.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "47f3a656-f0bc-5501-a979-06d1b6cd331d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ffeba9c-70a6-5328-9dc3-5020e0313ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333595} {:answer/id #uuid "4eafe18b-8c02-5577-a960-94ba6f971ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333711} {:answer/id #uuid "7213fed3-dd70-59f5-9da6-eb714e406490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334021} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc0b081-37ac-56aa-8ceb-99b894130c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334379} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb4516a-8d82-5f18-ae27-7aaf1618cf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333595} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0192dd7-c731-5b69-8d4c-3c9bab7026de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "555c1e17-369e-580d-8f06-901f476b3eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-65064" :question/problem "![](images/45/455df038e550d78fc29463c1a2e22dd44838d24af7428a824f9046fecc1ac1db.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/44/44a555d222e3f6ff85208e8722a728ad240c558de7982d2a480bf9ddfb23ac6f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{15}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a6cbd5b1-b78d-5262-a311-97b451bde36f"}] :activity/title "Using the Pythagorean Identity in the First Quadrant" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561575}]}
